---
// src/pages/website.astro — the post about the site itself: what it is made of, and how its robots are drawn.
// The figure (public/site-walk.webp, and site-walk-tall.webp for narrow pages) is eight frames of the runner on the
// resume page, taken from that page.
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const title = 'This Website';
const publishDate = 'April 11, 2025';
const updated = 'November 14, 2026';
---
<Layout title={`${title} - Project`} active="projects" description="How this site is put together: static pages built with Astro, with robots that are drawn live in the browser and one that walks on inverse kinematics.">
  <main class="page">
    <article class="wrap post">
      <header class="page-head">
        <div>
          <p class="kicker"><a href="/#projects">Projects</a> / website</p>
          <h1 class="page-title">{title}</h1>
          <p class="page-caption">
            A static site whose robots are drawn live in the browser. The ones on the home page play motions from my
            own training runs. The one on the resume page has no recording of a walk to play, so it works one out.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="page-meta meta">
          <span>{publishDate}</span>
          <span>Updated {updated}</span>
          <span>Astro · TypeScript · three.js</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="zone">
        <figure class="fig">
          <div class="fig-frame">
            <!-- The same eight frames: in one row on a wide page, in two rows of four on a narrow one. -->
            <picture>
              <source media="(max-width: 700px)" srcset="/site-walk-tall.webp" width="1400" height="1144" />
              <img width="2400" height="484" src="/site-walk.webp" alt="Eight frames of a white humanoid robot walking along a dotted line on the floor: its heel lands, its body passes over the standing foot, the foot behind rolls onto its toe and leaves the floor, and the other leg swings through" />
            </picture>
          </div>
          <figcaption>
            One stride of the walk on the resume page, a frame every 0.12 seconds. No stride is played back: each frame
            is worked out from where the feet are planted.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">01</p>
          <h2>What it is</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The site is a set of static pages, built with <a href="https://astro.build">Astro</a> and served by GitHub
          Pages. There is no server behind it and no content system: a page is a file, and so is a post like this one.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every page is set in one small style. White paper, one typeface with its code face for labels, neutral
          grays, and a single black pill for whatever is selected. Color is kept for meaning: amber is a reference,
          such as a path a robot has been asked to follow, and a translucent orange figure is where a robot will be
          next.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">02</p>
          <h2>The robots are drawn, not filmed</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The humanoid at the top of the home page is a Unitree G1, rendered with
          <a href="https://threejs.org">three.js</a>. Its motions are rollouts of the policy from
          <a href="/humanoid-on-a-macbook">Training a Humanoid on a MacBook</a>, stored in the model's file: a dance,
          a run, a fight, and a fall it gets up from. The orange figures beside it are its own next poses. On a first
          visit it starts on the floor and gets up, and dragging the stage turns the view around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The section titles work the same way at a smaller scale. The word is ordinary type, the robots of that
          section stand on it, and the scroll moves them. The page for an address that does not exist has the robot
          falling against the digits of <a href="/404">404</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each figure is drawn only while it is on screen. Without WebGL, or for a visitor who asks for reduced
          motion, the page shows the type or a picture and nothing is lost.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">03</p>
          <h2>A walk that is worked out</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The <a href="/resume">resume</a> is a timeline with a G1 on it that goes wherever the pointer is. The motions
          stored with the model have no walk among them, and a run played slowly is a slow jog. So the walk is
          computed, the way a walking robot computes one:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>A gait clock lifts the feet in turn.</li>
          <li>
            Each foot is set down where its hip is about to be, which is Raibert's rule, and then stays exactly where
            it was put. It rolls from its heel onto its toe as the body passes over it.
          </li>
          <li>
            The pelvis rides as high as the legs allow: lowest when both feet are down, highest over the standing leg.
          </li>
          <li>
            The six joints of each leg are solved by inverse kinematics on every frame, with damped least squares on
            the leg's Jacobian.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Standing still is the same machine with nothing to do. A small move of the pointer only shifts its weight,
          and turning to face the viewer is the same machine stepping around. Over a longer way it breaks into a jog,
          which is one stride of the recorded run closed into a loop. The walk hands over to the jog in step, and
          takes it back by planting whichever foot is down.
        </p>
        <p>
          The two quadrupeds behind the line stand on the same kind of machinery, and the arm on one of them reaches
          for the runner as it goes by.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">04</p>
          <h2>The pictures</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The pictures on the publication cards are renders. They use the robots' own models, the Unitree Go2 and G1,
          in Blender, with the same orange ghosts laid over them, and no backdrop, so they sit directly on the page.
          One command draws them all again.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="post-sec">
        <header class="post-sec-head">
          <p class="kicker">05</p>
          <h2>Before this</h2>
        </header>
        <div class="prose">
        <p>
          My first site was a Google Site: quick to make, free, and bland. The first version of this one, in April
          2025, was my first project in Astro, and it was built around frosted glass: blurred, translucent panels over
          bright shapes. In 2026 it was redrawn in the paper style it has now.
        </p>
        <p>
          The header's power button still restarts the site from a boot menu, where there is a safe-graphics mode and
          a memory test.
        </p>
        <p>
          The source is on <a href="https://github.com/VittorioPastore/VittorioPastore.github.io">GitHub</a>.
        </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </main>
</Layout>
